  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1232 PM MDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nTuesday begins the long term period with the center of the monsoon \nhigh still lingering along the NM/CO border near 595dm. With \nslightly weakening suppression beneath the ridge, a slightly \nhealthier crop of afternoon thunderstorms is favored to again \ndevelop over the western and northern mountains. Slow storm motions \nwill drift cells over surrounding highlands and lower elevations \nlate afternoon and evening. \n\nWednesday sees the high recenter along the Gulf Coast with a notable \ntap of monsoonal moisture pushing northward from the Gulf of CA into \nthe Desert Southwest. PWs rise to near seasonal normals around 1.00\" \nallowing for a much healthier crop of afternoon thunderstorms to \ndevelop mainly along and west of the Continental Divide. Storm \nmotions will favor a SSW/NNE motion over the western half to two-\nthirds of the state. Locally heavy rainfall from these storms will \nthreaten flash flooding in poorly drained areas and recent burn \nscars. This weather pattern of monsoonal moisture from the EPAC and \nGulf of CA being pushed into the Desert Southwest along the western \nperiphery of the high along the Gulf Coast continues Thursday and \ninto next weekend. Daily rounds of robust and healthy afternoon \nconvection will continue to favor mainly the western half to two-\nthirds of NM. High temperatures will also trend lower during this \ntime, to near or below normal across the Land of Enchantment.",
